Overview

This vignette shows how to:

  1. Add a test sample to MESOMICS MOFA inputs,
  2. Run MOFA2 and ParetoTI,
  3. Plot per-model test sample.

Example data paths

```{r}
mofa_dir <- system.file("extdata", package = "MESOMICS")
meso_csv <- system.file("extdata", "MESOMICS_latent_factors.csv", package = "MESOMICS")
test_csv <- system.file("extdata", "test-normalised-gene_count.csv", package = "MESOMICS")

stopifnot(meso_csv != "", test_csv != "")


out_dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "mesomics-vignette")
dir.create(out_dir, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
out_dir
```

## Step 0: Set Up Python Environment

Before using `MESOMICS`, make sure you have the required Python environment.

Open your terminal and run:

```bash
conda env create -f mofa_env.yml
conda activate mofa_env
which python
# For example: /home/user/miniconda3/envs/mofa_env/bin/python
```

## Step 1: add_sample_to_mofa()

```{r}
print('Starting Step 1')
MESOMICS::add_sample_to_mofa(
  test_matrix_path = test_csv,
  mofa_dir         = mofa_dir,
  value_data_types = "D_exprB_MOFA",
  outdir           = out_dir
)

```

## Step 2: run_mofa() with basilisk
```{r}
print('Starting Step 2')
MESOMICS::run_mofa(
  inputs_dir       = out_dir,   # <— where Step 1 wrote .RData
  outdir           = out_dir   # <— where to write MOFA-<sample>.hdf5
)

```

## Step 3: per-model plots
```{r}
print('Starting Step 3')
MESOMICS::plot_test_samples(
  models_dir   = out_dir,     # <— where MOFA-*.hdf5 live
  MESOMICS.LFs = meso_csv
)

list.files(file.path(out_dir, "plots"), pattern = "\\.pdf$", full.names = TRUE)
```

## Step : All sample plot
```{r}
MESOMICS::plot_test_all_samples(
  models_dir   = out_dir,
  mesomics_csv = meso_csv,
  out_pdf      = file.path(out_dir, "plots", "all_test_samples.pdf")
)
```

## Pipeline wrapper for Step 1 to 3:
```{r}
MESOMICS::run_1to3(
  test_matrix_path = test_csv,
  mofa_dir         = mofa_dir,
  inputs_dir       = out_dir,
  models_dir       = out_dir,
  mesomics_csv     = meso_csv
)

```



